## 4. JPA vs JDBC

The following are the differences  between the JPA and JDBC.

|                                                      JPA                                                      |                                                     JDBC                                                     |
| :-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------: | :----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------: |
|    Object-relational mapping (ORM) to simplify database access by mapping Java objects to database tables     |                              Direct low-level database access using SQL queries                              |
|                       Less complex, as it abstracts the underlying database operations                        | More complex, as it requires direct SQL queries and manual mapping between Java objects and database tables  |
| Faster development, as developers can focus on Java objects and the framework handles the database operations | Slower development, as developers need to write and test SQL queries and handle database operations manually |
|                   Have a small performance overhead due to the additional abstraction layer                   |                    Have better performance due to direct control over database operations                    |

For a small and simple web application with only a few tables, JDBC may be a good choice due to direct database control and potentially better performance. For more complex applications with many object relationships, JPA and Hibernate can simplify development and maintenance by handling the ORM.In complex applications with many object relationships, managing the mapping between the objects and the database can be difficult with JDBC. JPA and Hibernate simplify this process by handling the object-relational mapping automatically.

